1:# ADR-0113: Human and Agent Analysis as First-Class Claim Sources
26:### 1. Actor taxonomy
28:Every claim in `intelligence_claims` carries an `actor` string with one of the following canonical shapes. The `actor` column is non-nullable. Provenance's `Actor` type ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §1) uses the same shape.
30:| Actor prefix | Shape | Examples | Authority |
33:| `user_removal` | `user_removal` | `user_removal` | User removed a value (tombstone) |
126:### 6. Agent trust ledger
145:- User accepts an agent claim via corroboration or explicit confirmation → `alpha += 1`.
146:- User contradicts an agent claim (retraction, correction, "wrong") → `beta += 1`.
150:`posterior_score` is the Beta(α, β) mean, recomputed on update. Agents with `posterior_score` below a configurable floor (default 0.45) have all claims auto-routed to the Analysis Inbox regardless of trust-and-corroboration gate outcome, until their score recovers.
189:A claim is content-addressed by `dedup_key = hash(entity_id, claim_type, field_path, normalized_claim_text)`. Normalization includes trimming, Unicode normalization, and type-specific canonicalization (e.g., dates to ISO, names to case-folded).
203:- The `Actor` enum gains the shapes in §1 as its canonical values.
215:- `actor` column on `intelligence_claims` with canonical values from §1.
226:- Agent trust dashboard — v1.5.0.
236:- **Agent claims are auditable by actor.** Per-agent-per-claim-type trust posterior is durable and visible. A misbehaving agent version is detectable without log archaeology.
246:- **Agent version bumps lose trust history.** A conservative choice; the alternative (carry trust across versions) is worse because it masks regressions. Mitigated by warming new-version trust from a prior-version prior when acceptable.
248:- **Agent trust ledger is per-claim-type.** Some agents produce many claim types; the ledger can fan out. Acceptable for v1.4.0 scope (≤10 agents × ≤20 claim types = 200 rows); will revisit if fan-out becomes a real problem.
253:- `DataSource` taxonomy unchanged. Actor and source are separate axes.
295:**Consolidation timing — 2026-04-20 update:** Per plan-eng-review AskUserQuestion #1 and the brownfield-as-greenfield founder commitment (D1), consolidation ships in v1.4.0 as a destructive migration alongside the `intelligence_claims` table creation. Original R1.3 deferred consolidation to v1.4.1 out of migration caution; that deferral is retracted. Rationale: at AI velocity + minimal user base, keeping three parallel tombstone mechanisms alive through v1.4.0 creates interop cost that exceeds consolidation cost. The interop is what's expensive; one-table-to-rule-them-all is cleaner.
315:### R1.5 Actor representation — align with ADR-0102 `Actor` enum
317:The original §1 declared actor as a string with canonical shapes. [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §1 defines `Actor` as an enum (`User | Agent | System`). These conflict.
322:pub enum ClaimActor {
323:    User,
324:    UserRemoval,
325:    Human { role: HumanRole, id: UserId },
326:    Agent { name: AgentName, version: AgentVersion },
327:    System { component: SystemComponent },
332:Serialization produces the same canonical strings the original §1 proposed. Rust code uses the enum; the column stores the rendered string. This preserves ADR-0102's typing while accepting that a columnar DB stores serialized forms.
