   233	
   234	DailyOS is a single-user local desktop app; there is no separate "admin" identity (Codex finding #32). Global maintenance is either:
   235	
   236	1. **Scheduled-worker invocation** — the built-in background worker runs `global` maintenance on a declared schedule (weekly, monthly). The schedule is static code; it is not user-triggered. The worker is the only `System` path that may invoke `global`.
   237	2. **User-confirmed invocation** — the app surfaces a `global` maintenance operation through a dedicated Settings panel ("Maintenance > Run Reconciliation"). Clicking the action opens a typed confirmation dialog that:
   238	   - Displays the ability name, planned blast radius, expected duration, and budget.
   239	   - Requires the user to type the ability name to confirm.
   240	   - Issues a single-use `ConfirmationToken` scoped to the ability, valid for 60 seconds.
   241	   - Is the only in-app path to invoke `global` maintenance from a user action.
   242	
   243	Agents (MCP) cannot invoke `global` maintenance under any circumstance, regardless of actor policy overrides. This is a hard floor enforced at the registry.
   244	
   245	**Blast radius validation.** Self-declaration is not trustworthy by itself (Codex finding #23). Services that accept mutation calls declare their mutation scope in their function signature (e.g., `entities.refresh_state(entity_id)` is `single_entity`; `entities.recompute_all()` is `global`). The maintenance ability wrapper computes the maximum declared scope of services called and compares it to the ability's declared `blast_radius`. If the ability declares `single_entity` but calls a `global`-scoped service, registration fails with `Error::BlastRadiusUnderdeclared`.
   246	
   247	### 8. Intent-and-Outcome Audit (not signals)
   248	
   249	Every maintenance ability writes two audit records — NOT signals (Codex finding #16). Signals feed intelligence fusion, health scoring, and propagation; they affect the product's understanding of the world. Maintenance audit records are operational metadata describing what the system did to itself. Mixing them would contaminate scoring and propagation.
